      "claim": "The Fourth Geneva Convention applies to the territories east of the 1949 Armistice Line occupied by Israel in 1967, regardless of the prior sovereign status of those territories.",
      "reasoning": "Article 2(2) of the Fourth Geneva Convention, as a rule of customary international humanitarian law, extends the Convention’s provisions to any occupation of territory of a High Contracting Party. Jordan was a High Contracting Party to the Convention and exercised de facto authority over the West Bank and East Jerusalem before the 1967 conflict. The Convention’s applicability is independent of questions of sovereignty, as confirmed by Article 4 of Additional Protocol I, which states that the application of the Conventions does not affect the legal status of the occupied territory. Therefore, the Fourth Geneva Convention is binding on Israel with respect to the occupied Palestinian territories.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "Neither the occupation of a territory nor the application of the Conventions and this Protocol shall affect the legal status of the territory in question."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The humanitarian protections embodied in the Geneva Conventions are recognized as applicable to the occupied Palestinian territories by the Rome Statute, reinforcing the applicability of the Fourth Geneva Convention.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8(2)(c) of the Rome Statute makes serious violations of Article 3 common to the four Geneva Conventions punishable in non‑international armed conflicts. By incorporating Common Article 3, the Statute acknowledges that the Geneva Conventions’ humanitarian norms are binding in the context of the occupied territories, supporting the view that the Fourth Geneva Convention’s provisions are applicable there.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(c)",
          "quoted_text": "In the case of an armed conflict not of an international character, serious violations of article 3 common to the four Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949, namely, any of the following acts committed against persons taking no active part in the hostilities..."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
